INT. MINIVAN - INSIDE AN AUTOMATIC CAR WASH TUNNEL - DAY

Dark suds slide down the windshield in sheets. Blue light, pink light, a roar of brushes. The Okafor-Lindgren family sits sealed inside a rocking cocoon: DESMOND, fifties, gripping the wheel at ten and two though the van is on rails; his wife PRUDENCE "PRU", fifties, turned sideways in the front seat; their daughter ROXANNE "ROXY", sixteen, wedged in back with her knees against her chest; and Pru's father BARTHOLOMEW "POP-POP", eighties, queen of the back seat, tucked in a plaid blanket and a hat.

DESMOND
Nobody touch the doors.

PRU
Nobody is touching the doors.

ROXY
Mom, I'm literally nowhere near the doors.

POP-POP
Is it raining?

PRU
No, Daddy, it's a car wash.

POP-POP
Whose car?

DESMOND
Ours.

POP-POP
I thought it was Desmond's.

DESMOND
It is mine. Ours. It's our car.

ROXY
Pop-Pop, you've been in a car wash before.

POP-POP
Not with a family in it.

PRU
Desmond, the foam's coming in the vent.

DESMOND
It is not coming in the vent.

ROXY
It's coming in the vent.

DESMOND
Roxy, don't help.

ROXY
I'm not helping, I'm reporting.

POP-POP
Roxanne, you look like your grandmother when you frown.

ROXY
Is that good?

POP-POP
She frightened me in the nineteen-sixties and I married her.

PRU
Daddy, we've heard.

DESMOND
Pru, tell your father not to say "frightened me" while I'm in a tunnel.

PRU
He can say what he wants.

POP-POP
I'm eighty-four.

ROXY
You're eighty-four and you're the reason we're in here. You said the van smelled like soup.

POP-POP
It did smell like soup.

DESMOND
It smelled like Pru's lasagne.

PRU
Excuse me?

DESMOND
I like the lasagne!

PRU
You said it smelled.

DESMOND
Everything smells! It's a van!

ROXY
Dad, the sign says "Put in neutral."

DESMOND
I know what it says.

PRU
Are you in neutral?

DESMOND
I am in neutral.

POP-POP
Is it neutral?

DESMOND
It's neutral, Bartholomew.

POP-POP
Don't take that tone.

ROXY
Dad, you're supposed to be in neutral and also not touching the brakes.

DESMOND
I'm not touching the brakes!

PRU
Your foot is on the brake.

DESMOND
My foot is hovering.

ROXY
Hovering on the brake.

POP-POP
Is that why the brushes are stuck?

Everyone falls silent. The brushes slap the roof, steady, thudding, oddly intimate.

PRU
(softly)
Desmond.

DESMOND
It's fine.

ROXY
Is it fine?

DESMOND
It's fine!

The brushes lift. A wave of warm air blows through the tunnel. Pop-Pop begins to laugh, a wheeze that grows and grows, and one by one the others join him, helpless, water running down the glass.
